## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical iOS fundamentals: Codable data models, file-based persistence with atomic writes, and SwiftUI data flow. It also exercises UX concerns like debounce for responsiveness and accessibility considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Codable data model for Note with title, body, tags, and date
- File I/O in Documents with atomic write pattern
- SwiftUI state management using @Published and binding for filters
- Combine debouncing for search input
- Tag-based filtering logic and accessibility labeling

## Code Example
```javascript
import Foundation

struct Note: Codable, Identifiable {
  var id: UUID = UUID()
  var title: String
  var body: String
  var tags: [String]
  var date: Date = Date()
}

final class NotesStore: ObservableObject {
  @Published var notes: [Note] = []
  private let fileURL: URL = FileManager.default
    .urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask)[0]
    .appendingPathComponent("notes.json")

  init() { load() }

  func load() {
    guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: fileURL) else { return }
    if let loaded = try? JSONDecoder().decode([Note].self, from: data) {
      notes = loaded
    }
  }

  func save() {
    let tempURL = fileURL.appendingPathExtension("tmp")
    guard let data = try? JSONEncoder().encode(notes) else { return }
    try? data.write(to: tempURL)
    try? FileManager.default.replaceItemAtURL(fileURL, withItemAtURL: tempURL)
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to thousands of notes while keeping memory usage low?
- How would you test atomic write correctness and debounce behavior?
- How would you migrate to Core Data or add cloud sync later?

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates design of a scalable, non-destructive editing pipeline, leveraging Swift concurrency, Core Image, and memory-aware rendering. Also tests undo/redo, testability, and accessibility considerations in a production-like feature.

## Key Concepts
- Swift concurrency and actors for safe state sharing
- Core Image for GPU-accelerated filters
- Non-destructive editing via composable filter passes
- Undo/redo through graph state snapshots
- Memory management with tile-based rendering and lazy recomputation
- Testability: unit tests for graph evaluation; integration tests for export
- Accessibility hooks for UI feedback

## Code Example
```swift
// Skeleton: Filter protocol and graph actor
actor FilterGraph {
  struct Node { var id: String; var filter: CIFilter }
  var nodes: [Node] = []
  func applyAll(base: CIImage) async -> CIImage {
    var current = base
    for n in nodes {
      n.filter.setValue(current, forKey: kCIInputImageKey)
      if let out = n.filter.outputImage {
        current = out
      }
    }
    return current
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to handle very large images on memory-constrained devices?
- What trade-offs exist between tile-based rendering vs full-image compositing, and how would you test them?

## Why This Is Asked
